    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Junior Caminero** headlining the roster as our strongest play (81.92). His matchup against Gibson is the primary driver, as the 5.02 SP_xERA and favorable park factors provide a massive ceiling for his power-speed profile. Similarly, **Trea Turner** (74.82) and **Kyle Schwarber** (73.70) are high-priority starts against Buehler; while the opposing arm possesses name recognition, his current metrics suggest a prime opportunity for this duo to exploit his shaky peripherals. **Christian Yelich** (74.86) also grades as an elite start, benefiting from a high order position and a favorable dynamic platoon split against May, keeping him locked into the top of our offensive rotation.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involves **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (67.36). While he remains a strong start due to his leadoff duties and dynamic platoon advantage, we have adjusted his projection downward slightly to account for an elite catcher pop-time (1.86s), which suppresses his stolen base equity\u2014our most precious resource. He remains our primary focus for speed, but the proximity of **Yordan Alvarez** (57.40) to the bench line is notable; despite facing an elite arm in deGrom, **Yordan Alvarez** maintains an elite xwOBA that prevents us from sitting him, even when the matchup is suboptimal. \n\nRegarding the bench, **Caleb Durbin**, **Hyeseong Kim**, **Gary Sanchez**, and **Zac Veen** remain sidelined due to roster status or minor league assignments. **Zac Veen** is currently active with Albuquerque, keeping him off the board entirely. We are monitoring the weather closely, but the primary risk today remains the volatility of the matchups for **Dillon Dingler** (41.48) and **Casey Schmitt** (49.87). Both are hovering near our \"marginal\" floor; **Dillon Dingler** specifically struggles against Soriano\u2019s ability to neutralize base-stealing threats, making his efficiency score lower than his standard 